What the program actually does: it reads one integer with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, stuffs it into a [38;5;10mhouse_t[0m alongside [38;5;10mbedrooms = 3[0m and [38;5;10mbathrooms = 2.0[0m, then hexdumps the struct's raw memory. So the output is always 32 hex chars plus a newline, e.g. [38;5;10m01000000030000000000000000000040[0m for input [38;5;10m1[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two details that drove the implementation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The struct dump is a memory-layout observation, not a logical one. On x86-64 SysV the fields sit at offsets 0, 4, and 8 with size 16 and no padding holes, and [38;5;10m= {0}[0m zeroes the object first, so the byte image is fully determined. I reproduced it by writing each field's little-endian bytes into a zeroed [38;5;10m[u8; 16][0m rather than transmuting a struct, which keeps it in safe Rust. This does bake in the LP64 little-endian assumption — the same assumption the C output already depends on.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Failed and out-of-range [38;5;10mscanf[0m conversions are preserved rather than fixed. On a matching failure or EOF, [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initial [38;5;10m0[0m, so [38;5;10mdriver(0)[0m runs — no error is reported, matching the C. For out-of-range input, glibc accumulates into a [38;5;10mlong[0m with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m-style saturation and then truncates to [38;5;10mint[0m; that is undefined behavior per ISO C, but I mirrored the observed glibc behavior, so [38;5;10m4294967296[0m yields [38;5;10m0[0m and [38;5;10m2147483648[0m yields [38;5;10m0x80000000[0m.[0m[0m
